#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186151933 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "624215ff-efdc-5b12-8d61-9d66371672d9" :knowledge-point/title "Determining a Valid Region to Define a Double Integral Over a Non-Rectangular Domain"}] :question/id #uuid "80f011ae-c472-5dfa-8303-70396ecf51fd" :question/math-academy-id "e-6996" :question/problem "![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14050897/assets/7325a35720157e2331ade1b9abd042f90030361c1560dd3bf98de08cc495e4c4.png)\n\nConsider the region $D$ shown above. If \n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14050897/assets/d1457393a0b919a208da95e0b819b50a83847d2a5dfbb9c4c81c4c457a2cd467.png)\nwhich of the following could be the definition of the rectangular domain $R?$\n\n- $R=[-2,2] \\times [-2,1]$\n\n- $R=[-2,1] \\times [-2,2]$\n\n- $R=[-1,1] \\times [-1,1]$" :question/worked-solution "Notice that if $R$ covers $D$ completely and we define \n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14050897/assets/8e0d0d508d61352edf461f147cc561a527f6b3191e6c6b11d070eedf1e6572e3.png)\n\nthen, we obtain\n\n$$\n\\iint\\limits_D (x^2-y^2) \\: \\text{d}A = \\iint\\limits_R f(x,y) \\: \\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given rectangles.\n\n- Rectangles I and II cover the region $D$ completely.\n\n- Rectangle III does not cover the region $D.$\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"I and II only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186152745 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "624215ff-efdc-5b12-8d61-9d66371672d9" :knowledge-point/title "Determining a Valid Region to Define a Double Integral Over a Non-Rectangular Domain"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "9033537c-6596-540e-9bd3-d1cbeeabd1c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151381} {:answer/id #uuid "e565a422-78a5-5156-95c3-37f3e40e3ef7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151382} {:answer/id #uuid "2784e1ac-6059-539c-84d8-e18877b6df64" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I, II, and III" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151383} {:answer/id #uuid "394eb603-5869-540e-a54b-b403c5a2c6f3" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151384} {:answer/id #uuid "704cc418-5f2d-5e6f-a447-641d3ab76e51" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151385}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "9033537c-6596-540e-9bd3-d1cbeeabd1c8" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151381} :answer-field/id #uuid "4cc27ba3-e52d-57f2-a804-44b6bc1596df" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186151380}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045465 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "0e648a84-4fc0-5e89-9abf-3bfad9d2cf64" :question/math-academy-id "q-80266" :question/problem "Let $D$ be a finite region enclosed by the curve ${x}^{2}+\\frac{{y}^{2}}{4}=1.$ If \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}xy, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D\\end{aligned}\\,\\text{and}\\,{∬}_{D}xy\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A,\n$$\n\nwhich of the following could be the definition of the rectangular domain $R?$\n\n- $R=[-2,2]\\times [-1,1]$\n- $R=[-1,1]\\times [-2,2]$\n- $R=[-2,2]\\times [-2,2]$" :question/worked-solution "First, let's draw our region $D:$\n\n![](/home/jake/Developer/Course_Academy/reference/mathacademy/question-capture-workers/multivariable/14050897/assets/736575dceb6969f8b45e4038f702ebc84347ab66a1a8e5ea5103840ddd61ba31.png)\n\nNotice that if $R$ covers $D$ completely and we define \n\n$$\nf(x,y)={\\begin{aligned}xy, & (x,y)\\in R\\cap D \\\\ 0, & (x,y)\\in R∖D,\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nwe obtain\n\n$$\n{∬}_{D}xy\\,\\text{d}A={∬}_{R}f(x,y)\\,\\text{d}A.\n$$\n\nWith that in mind, let's examine each of the given rectangles.\n\n- Rectangle I does not cover the region $D.$\n\n- Rectangles II and III cover the region $D$ completely.\n\nTherefore, the correct answer is \"II and III only.\""}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186328744 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:knowledge-point/id #uuid "624215ff-efdc-5b12-8d61-9d66371672d9" :knowledge-point/title "Determining a Valid Region to Define a Double Integral Over a Non-Rectangular Domain"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "468ef737-2820-5047-a9db-60f8100c852d" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II and III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328746} {:answer/id #uuid "ac1489c9-537b-5383-87d8-0caaad2d5cb1" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328747} {:answer/id #uuid "453ed57b-0f79-54c7-8419-74c267e51a52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328748} {:answer/id #uuid "39368406-f5e2-5f0f-b061-594f5de5fd32" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "I and II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328749} {:answer/id #uuid "a9d1a784-7f6b-5c4a-a244-3ebd63dbabd7" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "II only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328750}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "453ed57b-0f79-54c7-8419-74c267e51a52" :answer/type {:db/ident :answer.type/text} :answer/value "III only" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328748} :answer-field/id #uuid "155b9131-d31f-566c-8b32-b917dc6f8434" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186328745}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045463 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "7f020700-6aa9-5008-bb57-88129d05abec" :question/math-academy-id "q-86860" :que